      title: "The Magistrates",
      body: `## Res publica

The Romans called their state the **res publica**, "the public thing," the common affairs of the people, as opposed to the **res privata**, private affairs. The phrase did not mean a particular form of government; Cicero could speak of a res publica ruled by a king. But in Roman memory, the res publica began with the expulsion of the kings in 509 BCE, and it came to mean, above all, a state in which no single man held permanent power. Its formal name, inscribed on standards, monuments, and coins, was **SPQR**: *Senatus Populusque Romanus*, "the Senate and the Roman People."

The Republic had no written constitution. Its institutions grew over centuries, through law, custom, and conflict, and were governed by the **mos maiorum**, "the custom of the ancestors," the body of tradition that defined how things were properly done. In this lesson, we look at how the Republic worked in its mature form, in the third and second centuries BCE, and then at how it came to be that way.

## The consuls

At the head of the Republic were two **consuls**, elected each year. They held **imperium**, the supreme power of command, in war and at home, which the kings had held before them: the power to command armies, to administer justice, to summon the senate and the assemblies, and to propose laws. Their power was symbolized by the **fasces**, carried before them by twelve attendants called **lictors**. Within the city, the axe was removed from the fasces, a sign that a citizen could not be executed without trial; outside the city, the axe was restored.

Two principles limited the consuls' power. The first was **annuality**: they held office for only a year, after which they returned to private life, and could be prosecuted for their actions in office. The second was **collegiality**: there were two of them, each with equal power, and each could veto the other (the right of **intercessio**). These principles, the Romans believed, prevented the return of monarchy. The consuls gave their names to the year: Romans dated events not by numbered years but by the names of the consuls, "in the consulship of Lucius Aemilius Paullus and Gaius Terentius Varro." The list of consuls, the **fasti**, was the backbone of Roman chronology.

## The other magistrates

Below the consuls was a hierarchy of other magistrates, all elected for a year, and all collegial:

- **Praetors** also held imperium. The first praetor, created in **367 BCE**, administered justice in the city; a second, the **praetor peregrinus**, created around **242 BCE**, handled cases involving foreigners. As Rome acquired provinces, more praetors were created to govern them; by Sulla's time, there were eight. Each year, the city praetor issued an **edict** setting out the legal remedies he would grant, and over time these edicts developed into a sophisticated body of law.
- **Aediles** supervised the city: streets, markets, the grain supply, public buildings, and public games. The games gave ambitious men an opportunity to win popularity by spending lavishly from their own pockets.
- **Quaestors** managed the treasury and the finances of armies and provinces.
- **Censors**, two of them, were elected every five years for eighteen months. They conducted the **census**, registering citizens and assessing their property; they let out contracts for public works and the collection of taxes; and they revised the roll of the senate, removing members guilty of misconduct. The censorship, though it held no imperium, was the most prestigious office, usually held by former consuls.

## The cursus honorum

By the second century BCE, the offices had been arranged into a fixed order, the **cursus honorum**, "the course of honors," with minimum ages fixed by the **lex Villia annalis** of **180 BCE**: quaestor, then (optionally) aedile, then praetor, then consul. Later, a two-year interval was required between offices, and in Sulla's time, the minimum ages were fixed at about thirty for the quaestorship, thirty-nine for the praetorship, and forty-two for the consulship. Re-election to the consulship within ten years was forbidden, though the rule was often broken in emergencies.

The ladder narrowed at the top. Each year, there were (in the late Republic) twenty quaestors, eight praetors, and two consuls. Competition for office was fierce, and the consulship was the supreme prize, conferring on a man and his descendants the status of **nobilis**, "known," a noble. A man who reached the consulship without consular ancestors was called a **novus homo**, a "new man"; the most famous was **Cicero**, consul in 63 BCE.

## Promagistrates

As Rome's wars and provinces multiplied, there were not enough magistrates to command all the armies and govern all the territories. The Romans solved this by extending the command of a magistrate beyond his year of office, as a **proconsul** or **propraetor**, acting "in place of" a consul or praetor. The first such extension, or **prorogation**, was made in 326 BCE, for the consul **Quintus Publilius Philo**, so that he could complete the siege of Naples (Livy 8.23). By the late Republic, most provinces were governed by former consuls and praetors, for terms of a year or more. This would prove dangerous: a man with a long command, a loyal army, and the wealth of a province could become more powerful than the Republic itself.

## The dictator

In emergencies, the consuls could appoint a **dictator**, a single magistrate with supreme power, for a maximum of **six months**. The dictator was attended by twenty-four lictors, and appointed a deputy, the **Master of the Horse** (*magister equitum*). The most famous was **Lucius Quinctius Cincinnatus**, who, according to the legend, was plowing his small farm across the Tiber in **458 BCE** when envoys arrived to tell him that he had been named dictator to rescue a Roman army trapped by the Aequi. He wiped off the sweat and dust, put on his toga, defeated the enemy in sixteen days, celebrated a triumph, resigned his power, and returned to his plow (Livy 3.26–29). The dictatorship fell out of use after 202 BCE, until it was revived, in a new and terrible form, by **Sulla** and then **Caesar**.

The memory of Cincinnatus inspired later republics. When **George Washington** resigned his commission at the end of the American Revolution and returned to his farm at Mount Vernon, he was hailed as a new Cincinnatus, and the officers of the Continental Army founded a hereditary association called the **Society of the Cincinnati**, after which the city of Cincinnati, Ohio, was named.

> **Pause and consider.** Cincinnatus held absolute power for sixteen days and gave it up to return to his plow. Why did later republics value this story so much? What does it suggest about the danger they most feared?`,
