      title: "The Senate and the Assemblies",
      body: `## The senate

The **senate** (**senatus**, from *senex*, "old man") was the council of the Republic. It had about **three hundred** members in the middle Republic, enlarged to six hundred by Sulla. Senators were not elected to the senate; they were enrolled by the censors, who chose them from among former magistrates. Membership was for life, unless a censor removed a man for misconduct. In practice, the senate was composed of the men who had held, or would hold, the magistracies: the governing class of the Republic.

Formally, the senate was only an advisory body. It could not pass laws. It met when summoned by a magistrate, and issued **decrees** (**senatus consulta**), which were technically advice to the magistrates. But its advice was almost always followed, and in practice it controlled the most important matters of state: **foreign policy**, including the reception of embassies and the making of treaties; **finance**, including the allocation of money to magistrates and armies; the assignment of **provinces** and **commands**; and the supervision of **religion**. Its authority (**auctoritas**) rested on the experience, prestige, and wealth of its members, and on the respect for the mos maiorum.

Debate in the senate followed a strict order of precedence. The presiding magistrate asked the senators for their opinions in order of rank: first the **princeps senatus**, the senior senator, then the former consuls, the former praetors, and so on. Junior senators rarely spoke; they voted by moving to one side of the chamber or the other. Senators wore a tunic with a broad purple stripe (*latus clavus*) and special shoes. Their main meeting place was the **Curia Hostilia** in the Forum, traditionally built by King Tullus Hostilius.

The senate's dignity impressed foreigners. When **Cineas**, the envoy of King Pyrrhus of Epirus, returned from Rome in 280 BCE, he is said to have told the king that the senate seemed to him "an assembly of many kings" (Plutarch, *Pyrrhus* 19).

## The assemblies

The Roman people exercised their power through **assemblies**, which elected the magistrates, passed laws, declared war, and, in the early period, judged capital cases. But Roman assemblies differed from the Athenian assembly that you studied in ANH 101 in several important ways:

- They could meet only when **summoned by a magistrate**, and could vote only on proposals that he put before them. They could not debate or amend them. Debate took place beforehand, in informal meetings called **contiones**, where magistrates and others addressed the crowd.
- Citizens voted not as individuals, but in **groups**. Each group's vote was decided by the majority of its members, and the majority of groups decided the issue.
- Voting took place in Rome. Citizens who lived far from the city, as most did once Roman citizenship had spread through Italy, could rarely attend.

There were several assemblies, with different organizations.

**The comitia centuriata**, the "assembly by centuries," met on the **Campus Martius**, outside the sacred boundary of the city, because it was originally the army assembled. It elected the **consuls, praetors, and censors**, declared war, and passed some laws. It was organized into **193 centuries**, grouped by wealth. The **equites**, the cavalry, who were the richest citizens, had **18** centuries; the **first class**, the wealthiest infantry, had **80**; the second, third, and fourth classes had 20 each, the fifth 30; there were 4 centuries of engineers and musicians; and all the citizens too poor to be registered in the classes, the **proletarii** or **capite censi** ("counted by head"), formed a single century. The centuries voted in order of wealth, beginning with the equites and the first class, and voting stopped once a majority of 97 was reached. Since the equites and the first class together had 98 centuries, they could decide the result without the poorer centuries ever voting, if they agreed. The system was reformed in the third century BCE, so that the first class had 70 centuries, but the wealthy still dominated. Cicero approved: the system ensured, he said, that "the greatest number should not have the greatest power" (*Republic* 2.39).

**The comitia tributa**, the "assembly by tribes," was organized by the **tribes**, territorial divisions of the citizen body, of which there were eventually **35**: 4 urban and 31 rural. It elected the lower magistrates, the quaestors and curule aediles, and passed laws. Each tribe had one vote. Because the urban poor were concentrated in the 4 urban tribes, while the wealthier rural landowners, who could afford to travel to Rome, dominated the 31 rural tribes, this assembly also favored the propertied.

**The concilium plebis**, the "council of the plebs," was organized by tribes like the comitia tributa, but included only plebeians, and was presided over by the tribunes of the plebs. It elected the tribunes and plebeian aediles, and passed resolutions called **plebiscita**, which, after 287 BCE, had the force of law for the whole people. By the late Republic, most legislation was passed in this assembly.

There was also an ancient **comitia curiata**, organized in thirty **curiae**, which by the late Republic had become a formality, represented by thirty lictors, confirming the imperium of magistrates and certain adoptions.

## Was Rome a democracy?

Was the Roman Republic a democracy? In one sense, the people were sovereign: they elected all the magistrates and passed all the laws. In 1998, the British historian **Fergus Millar** argued, in *The Crowd in Rome in the Late Republic*, that Rome had important democratic features, and that the speeches made to the people in the Forum were the heart of Roman politics. Most historians, however, see the Republic as essentially **oligarchic** or **aristocratic**: the people could choose only among candidates from a narrow elite, vote only on proposals made by magistrates, and the voting system was weighted toward the wealthy. The truth may be that Rome was an aristocratic republic in which the people, nonetheless, had real and sometimes decisive power, and in which the elite had to compete for popular favor.

> **Pause and consider.** In the comitia centuriata, the richest citizens voted first, and often decided the result before the poor voted at all. Cicero approved. What might be said for and against such a system?`,
