      title: "Patricians, Plebeians, and the First Secession",
      body: `## Two orders

From the beginning of the Republic, the Roman citizen body was divided into two **orders** (**ordines**): the **patricians** and the **plebeians**. The patricians were a small group of clans (*gentes*), such as the Cornelii, the Claudii, the Fabii, the Valerii, and the Aemilii, who claimed a hereditary right to hold the magistracies and the major priesthoods, and to take the **auspices**, the consultation of the gods' will through birds and signs. The name patrician derives from *patres*, "fathers," the name of the senators. The **plebeians** (**plebs**, "the multitude") were everyone else: not only the poor, but also wealthy families who were not patrician.

How the patriciate arose is uncertain. The Romans believed that Romulus created it by choosing a hundred senators. Modern scholars have suggested that it formed gradually under the kings or in the early Republic, as a group of families monopolized the priesthoods and closed their ranks. Marriage between patricians and plebeians was, at least for a time, forbidden.

## The Conflict of the Orders

The history of the first two centuries of the Republic, as the Roman tradition told it, was dominated by a long struggle between the two orders, known as the **Conflict of the Orders** (from about 494 to 287 BCE). The plebeians had two main grievances: **economic** and **political**.

The economic grievances were **debt** and **land**. Many small farmers, called up for military service while their fields went untended, fell into debt. Under the harsh law of **nexum**, a debtor who could not pay could be bound to his creditor, and forced to work for him, in conditions close to slavery. And the land conquered from Rome's neighbors, the **ager publicus**, "public land," was occupied by the rich, while the poor got little.

The political grievance was the patricians' **monopoly of office**. The plebeians who fought in Rome's armies, and the wealthy plebeians who had the means and ambition to lead, were excluded from the magistracies, the priesthoods, and, it seems, from the senate's inner circle.

## The first secession

According to the tradition, in **494 BCE**, the plebeians, burdened by debt, and outraged by the treatment of debtors, refused to serve in the army. Livy tells of an old soldier who appeared in the Forum, ragged and filthy, and told how, while he was fighting in the Sabine war, his farm had been burned and his cattle driven off; he had fallen into debt, lost his land, and been dragged off by his creditor to a workhouse, where he was flogged. He showed the scars on his back, beside the scars of battle on his chest (Livy 2.23). The crowd rioted.

When the consuls led the army out against the enemy, the soldiers withdrew in a body to the **Sacred Mount** (*Mons Sacer*), three miles from Rome (some sources say the Aventine), and established themselves there, refusing to return. This was the first **secession of the plebs**: a mass withdrawal, a kind of general strike, by which the plebeians, who were the bulk of the army, brought the state to a halt.

## The fable of the belly

The senate sent **Menenius Agrippa**, an eloquent man of plebeian origin, to negotiate. He told the plebeians a fable (Livy 2.32). Once, the parts of the body rebelled against the **belly**, which, they said, sat idle in the middle of the body, enjoying the food that they worked to provide. The hands refused to carry food to the mouth, the mouth refused to take it, the teeth refused to chew. But soon all the members grew weak, and they realized that the belly too had its function, which was to nourish the whole body. So, Menenius said, the senate nourished the state, and the people could not live without it.

The fable is a classic statement of the **organic** theory of society, in which different classes, like the parts of a body, have different functions and depend on one another. Shakespeare put it in the mouth of Menenius in *Coriolanus*, where the citizens answer it with skepticism. It is a story told from the point of view of the belly.

## The tribunes of the plebs

The plebeians returned, but only after the patricians agreed to the creation of new officials, the **tribunes of the plebs** (*tribuni plebis*), to protect them. At first there were two, later ten. They were elected by the plebeians alone, in their own assembly, the concilium plebis. They were not magistrates of the whole people, but officials of the plebs, and their power rested on an oath sworn by the plebeians to kill anyone who harmed a tribune: they were **sacrosanct**, inviolable.

The tribunes had remarkable powers:

- **Auxilium**, "help": the right to protect any plebeian from the action of a magistrate, by physically intervening.
- **Intercessio**, the **veto**: the right to block any act of a magistrate, any decree of the senate, or any law proposed in the assemblies. (The Latin word *veto* means "I forbid.") A single tribune could bring the government to a halt.
- The right to summon the **concilium plebis** and propose resolutions to it.

The tribunate was an extraordinary institution: a power of obstruction placed in the hands of officials elected by one part of the people, against the government of the whole. In Cicero's dialogue *On the Laws*, his brother Quintus calls it a power born in sedition and made for sedition; Cicero, replying, defends it as a safety valve that restrained the plebs (3.19–26). In the later Republic, as we will see in lesson 4, the tribunate would become the instrument of reformers and demagogues such as the Gracchi.

The plebeians also created their own **aediles**, who looked after the temple of **Ceres**, the goddess of grain, on the Aventine, which became the center of the plebeian organization. The plebs, in other words, formed a **state within the state**, with its own assembly, its own officials, and its own sanctuary.

## Coriolanus

The tradition tells a story that shows the tension of these years. **Gaius Marcius Coriolanus**, a patrician war hero who had won his name by capturing the Volscian town of Corioli, opposed the distribution of grain to the plebs during a famine, saying they should first give up their tribunes. He was prosecuted by the tribunes and went into exile among the Volscians, whom he led against Rome. The city was saved only when his mother **Veturia** and his wife **Volumnia** came out to his camp and persuaded him to withdraw. "Mother, you have won a victory for Rome," he is said to have answered, "but you have lost your son" (Dionysius 8.54; Plutarch, *Coriolanus* 36; Livy 2.40 tells the scene differently). The story, which Shakespeare also dramatized, may be largely legendary, but it captures the danger of an aristocrat who scorns the people.

> **Pause and consider.** Menenius Agrippa compared the senate to the belly that nourishes the whole body. What does the fable assume about society? How might the plebeians have answered it?`,
